Teacher: an MLP 784 → 512 → 512 → 10 with dropout (about 670,000 parameters), trained on MNIST images 5,000 to 60,000. Student: 784 → 200 → 10 (about 159,000, a quarter of the size). Train three students with the same settings:
• hard labels only, on images 0 to 5,000 (the labelled set);
• distilled on the same 5,000 images: α × T² × KL(teacher's soft probabilities ‖ student's) + (1 − α) × cross-entropy with the hard labels, T = 4, α = 0.7;
• soft labels only, on all 60,000 images, using no true labels at all: the teacher labels them.

The honest result, averaged over 3 seeds: on the same 5,000 images, distillation did not beat hard labels here (about 91.7% against 92.2%). With an MLP on an easy dataset, the teacher's soft labels added little that the true labels didn't already give. But the third student, which learned only from the teacher on 60,000 unlabelled images, reached about 95.7%, more than 3 points higher.

That is distillation's main practical use: once you have a good teacher, it can label as much extra data as you like for free, and a small student learns its behaviour from that. The "dark knowledge" in soft labels matters more on harder tasks with many similar classes; always compare against the hard-label baseline rather than assuming it helps.

